---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-shade`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Shade',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Shade', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Shade: Do You Make Vitamin D Under a Tree?"
  description="How trees, umbrellas, buildings, and dappled shade affect UVB — scatter can deliver 30-50% of direct UVB, which matters more than most people think."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-shade", label: "Vitamin D and Shade" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Shade</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Shade isn't zero-UVB. Scattered UV from the sky reaches shaded skin, and dense
      tree canopy or a beach umbrella still transmits 30–50% of ambient UVB. This
      matters for both sunburn risk (more than you'd think) and vitamin D synthesis
      (still meaningful in shaded midday outdoor time).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The scatter physics</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      UV reaches skin via two paths: direct (from the solar disk) and diffuse
      (Rayleigh-scattered from the whole hemisphere of sky). Under shade, direct is
      blocked but diffuse continues. On a clear day, diffuse UV is roughly 40–60%
      of total ambient UV — that's what shade delivers to shielded skin.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Shade types and transmission</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Dense evergreen canopy</strong> — 5–15% UVB transmission.</li>
      <li><strong>Deciduous canopy in full leaf</strong> — 10–20%.</li>
      <li><strong>Dappled shade</strong> — 20–40% (highly variable; sunflecks can approach 100%).</li>
      <li><strong>Beach umbrella</strong> — 40–60%. Direct blocked, diffuse mostly through.</li>
      <li><strong>Pergola / gazebo</strong> — 30–50%.</li>
      <li><strong>Solid roof / awning</strong> — 5–15% (direct blocked, side-scattered UV).</li>
      <li><strong>Between buildings, urban shade</strong> — 30–60% depending on sky visibility.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Beach umbrella deception</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Studies of beach umbrellas (McKenzie 2017) found that shaded sunbathers still
      accumulated meaningful UVB exposure — enough that after 3–4 hours under an
      umbrella at a sunny beach, some subjects had light sunburns. Shade reduces
      exposure; it doesn't eliminate it. Sunscreen remains recommended for extended
      shade-only time in tropical or high-UV conditions.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Vitamin D synthesis in shade</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      An hour under a beach umbrella at midday tropical sun delivers roughly the
      vitamin D equivalent of 20–30 minutes in direct sun — still substantial.
      Sitting in dappled shade for an afternoon walking in a park produces
      meaningful synthesis. Shade is not a vitamin D-blocking strategy; direct
      sun-avoidance under bright sky still permits synthesis.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical takeaway</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Shade is not an on/off switch — it's a partial reduction.</li>
      <li>You can be in the shade all day at a sunny beach and still get sunburned; use sunscreen.</li>
      <li>You can be in dappled shade in a park and still make useful vitamin D.</li>
      <li>Full indoor time is different — see our <a href="/vitamin-d-and-windows" class="text-amber-700 underline">windows page</a>.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Shade seeking is a smart general sun-safety
      practice; don't assume it fully protects, but do use it in combination with
      sunscreen and clothing.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-shade" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
